"Play DRM-protected content" on Linux, the owner's decision: an opt-in setting, off by default, as Firefox does it.
chromium-component-updater-security-only.patch keeps every component off the network but the security ones; with this
patch Widevine's component (oimompecagnajdejgnnjijobebaeigek) joins them only while the local-state pref
component_updater::kArdiaPlayProtectedContentPref ("ardia.play_protected_content", registered with the component
updater's prefs, default false) is on. The app's settings set it; the engine reads it before the installed component is
read and follows its changes.
- Off: nothing is fetched (the update client never sees the component), an installed CDM's ComponentReady doesn't
  write the Linux hint file, and the hint file is deleted, so no Widevine CDM loads at the next start.
- Turned on: an update check for the component (MaybeThrottle, the public on-demand call, with its cooldown) fetches it
  from Google, and an installed one is hinted again. On Linux the CDM loads at startup, before the zygote is locked
  down, so it plays from the next start.

